the armel center in southern beirut  provides refugees with medical help as  well as a place to socialize and learn a  new trade so they can find work  mona is training to be a beautician and  hopes to open her own shop  one day  for now she says life is difficult  our husbands can't find work it's the  wives who have to look after the  families  mona fled to beirut from syria two years  ago now she lives near the center with  her four children  it's through local relief projects like  this that international aid reaches the  refugees  once they reach lebanon refugees are  largely left to themselves  unlike its neighbours lebanon has made a  point of not setting up camps to stop  refugees from settling in for the long  haul  in beirut germany's foreign minister  frank walter steinmeier is keen to  stress that his country has taken in  more than one hundred thousand syrian  refugees  that's more than any other country  outside the region  we need to share this challenge more  evenly there needs to be willingness  from european countries to do more for  syrian  refugees so far germany has spent over a  billion euros supporting syrian refugees  and their host countries  but that's not going to be enough as  long as the international community does  nothing to end the bloody conflict at  the root of this refugee crisis
 

